omycorrhizal fungi to cadmium uptake of poplars and willows from a heavily polluted soil</article-title><source>Plant Soil</source><year>2005</year><volume>277</volume><fpage>245</fpage><lpage>253</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b15-ijerph-06-00414"><label>15.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>G&#x000f6;hre</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Paszkowski</surname><given-names>U</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Contribution of the arbuscular mycorrhizal symbiosis to heavy metal Phytoremediation</article-title><source>Planta</source><year>2006</year><volume>223</volume><fpage>1115</fpage><lpage>1122</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16555102</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b16-ijerph-06-00414"><label>16.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Courbot</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Ruotolo</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Chalot</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Leroy</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>2004 Cadmium-responsive thiols in the ectomycorrhizal fungus Paxillus involutus</article-title><source>Appl. Environ. Microbiol</source><year>2004</year><volume>70</volume><fpage>7413</fpage><lpage>7417</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15574943</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b17-ijerph-06-00414"><label>17.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bruns</surname><given-names>TD</given-names></name><name><surname>Bidartondo</surname><given-names>MI</given-names></name><name><surname>Taylor</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Host Specificity in Ectomycorrhizal Communities: What Do the Exceptions Tell Us?</article-title><source>Integ. Comp. Biol</source><year>2002</year><volume>42</volume><fpage>352</f